WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.240
- [Narrator] New coronavirus variants

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.430
have been detected in over 40 countries,

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.030
including the UK, South
Africa, Nigeria and Brazil

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.410
where they first emerged.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.100
- Now health officials say a new threat

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.660
is identified in South Africa.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.530
- British officials imposed a
new lockdown across England.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.660
- Recent reports of a highly
contagious coronavirus variant

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.310
in the United States.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.520
- [Narrator] In the US, multiple states

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.200
have reported new variants

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.570
or versions of the virus with mutations

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.310
that could change how it spreads.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:31.780
New research suggests

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.380
that some of these variants
are highly transmissible

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.590
and at least one may reduce
how well vaccines work.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.160
With countries around the world

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.530
already facing escalating infections

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.370
and overwhelmed hospitals,

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:44.960
these mutations could make ending

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.460
the pandemic more difficult.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.293
We'll explain.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.580
Like other viruses,
the coronavirus spreads

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.690
by infecting cells and then
reproducing within them,

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.930
creating copies of itself that
spread throughout the body.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.638
As new versions are made,
the virus' genetic code,

00:01:01.638 --> 00:01:03.570
which holds the instructions

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.817
for building new virus
particles is copied over

00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:08.050
and over again.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.040
But the code isn't always
reproduced correctly.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.710
Small genetic mistakes or mutations

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.930
can create new viral variants.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.600
If the new version of
the virus has mutations

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.210
that allow it to spread faster

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.310
than the current most common variant,

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.150
it could lead to a surge in new cases.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.610
This is already happening in places

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.430
like the UK and South Africa

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.990
where some of the newest
ones were first found.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.820
Current data suggests that the UK variant

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.290
could be 50% to 70% more transmissible

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.430
but the research is still early.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.200
To determine how transmissible

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.930
and dangerous a virus mutation might be,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.310
scientists run a series
of experiments in the lab.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.740
The first step is genetic sequencing.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.010
- Genetic sequencing gets to the blueprint

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.180
of what the viruses are.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.040
We can use samples and data

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.960
from actual humans to figure out

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.480
what the genetics of the viruses are.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.990
- [Narrator] To do this,
scientists take a sample

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.730
of the mutated virus
and sequence its genes.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.590
Then computation programs

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.000
help scientists identify mutations.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.520
- Once we know what they are

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.270
and now we can see that oh well,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.926
this is an important population,

00:02:16.926 --> 00:02:18.930
we can start manipulating the virus.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.920
We can either start working
with clinical islets

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.020
of that virus or we can
actually make the strains.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.210
So putting those individual mutations

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.570
into the backbone of viruses
that we already have.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.210
- [Narrator] After sequencing the virus,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.300
scientists test the variant in a lab.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.500
- What we do is we compare
how well the virus replicates

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.910
in different types of cells.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:37.900
And so there are two ways.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.800
We will just do infections
and kind of measure the level

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.570
of the virus over time
in these cell types.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.620
And the other thing that
we'll do is a competition,

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.700
where we'll take 50% of the wild type,

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.630
the original strain and
50% of the mutant strain

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.960
and then compare how well they grow.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.780
If there's an advantage, we'll see that

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.282
that advantage 24 hours or after 48 hours,

00:02:58.282 --> 00:03:00.430
one of the two might out compete.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.910
- [Narrator] During lab experiments,

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:05.610
researchers can also test
whether human antibodies created

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.260
by infection or vaccines

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.340
are effective at fighting
the new form of the virus.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.650
After culture testing,

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.350
scientists typically
move on to animal models

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.430
and then sometimes to humans.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.940
Right now, the research is still early

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.650
but scientists are beginning
to get a clearer picture

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.820
of what these variants look like.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.420
From sequencing, they know the UK variant

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.100
has 17 key mutations.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.170
- These variants that we're seeing,

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.999
these changes give a small advantage

00:03:32.999 --> 00:03:34.580
in terms of the ability of the virus

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:35.950
to replicate or transmit.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.600
- [Narrator] This has a lot
to do with the spike protein,

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.160
which is critical to
how the virus spreads.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.850
A portion of the spike bonds to a receptor

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.460
on human cells like a
key fitting into a lock.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.230
This enables it to enter and infect.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.330
Scientists found that the
UK variant's spike proteins

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.590
have eight amino acid changes.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.790
Previous research appears to show

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.310
that some of these
changes may be increasing

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.390
the protein's ability

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.570
to cling onto and enter human cells.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.630
This could be making
it more transmissible,

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.100
which means a higher
vaccination rate may be needed

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.640
to reach herd immunity.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.030
Another big question is the virulence

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.530
or how harmful these new
versions of the virus could be.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.310
Scientists say that most
likely the new variants

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.740
don't cause a more
severe form of COVID-19.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.170
Another question is
whether the new versions

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.780
will show up on tests.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.230
Early data appears to show that most COVID

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:33.290
and antibody testing
will continue to work.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.738
- We should still be able
to tell if you have COVID.

00:04:35.738 --> 00:04:36.900
What's less clear is if we can tell

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.020
if you have a specific variant or not.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.370
- [Narrator] The most pressing issue

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.370
is whether vaccines
will still be effective.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.850
For the UK variant, it
appears that they will.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:49.240
However, for the variant
originating in South Africa,

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.550
preliminary data is
worrying some scientists.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.670
Researchers say that any of
the more transmissible variants

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.530
could eventually become dominant.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.430
- We've seen variants take over

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:02.800
in terms of the D614G mutation

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.070
and it wouldn't surprise
me if the South African

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.060
or the UK variant do a similar thing

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.670
where it becomes a dominant
variant around the world

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.520
just because of a small fitness advantage

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.880
or small changes that
make the virus more fit.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.520
- [Narrator] To try to stem the spread,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.500
certain countries like the UK

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.530
have already imposed
strict lockdown measures.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.442
- The number of patients
in hospitals in England

00:05:24.442 --> 00:05:28.640
is now 40% higher than
the first peak in April.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.620
It is inescapable that
the facts are changing

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.790
and we must change our response.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.840
And so we have no choice

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.270
but to return to a national lockdown.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.600
- [Narrator] While the new
variants are concerning,

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.240
experts say that current
prevention measures

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.540
should continue to work.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.000
- This isn't something that's
completing overcoming masks

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.860
or distancing and so if these measures

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.320
were put in place in more stricter,

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.470
more effective ways,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.730
it'll be just as effective,
if not more effective.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.220
And the outcome may be that
being a little bit more serious

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:02.250
about this will get
transmission as a whole

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.850
to be reduced and I think that's an ideal.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:07.640
- [Narrator] Scientists say
the coronavirus pandemic

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.130
could be on the cusp of a major shift

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:11.760
but more research is needed

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.520
to know how severe the impact

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.103
of the new variants will be.

00:06:15.944 --> 00:06:18.694
(dramatic music)

